export default "# Tintwork user guide\n\nTintwork is a layered photo editor that runs entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded: images, projects and autosaves stay on your device. It works best in Chrome or Edge on Windows with a mouse; other Chromium browsers, Firefox, Safari and touch devices also work.\n\n## Getting started\n\n- **Welcome screen**: open an image, start a blank canvas, pick a size preset (Full HD, square, 4:5, A4, 24 MP) or reopen a recent file. It appears whenever no document is open.\n- **Open** (Ctrl+O): JPEG, PNG (8- and 16-bit), WebP, GIF, BMP, Tintwork projects, layered PSD/PSB files and camera RAW files (DNG, CR2, CR3, NEF, ARW, RAF, ORF, RW2 and more). You can also drag files onto the window or paste an image (Ctrl+V). Opening several files gives each its own tab.\n- **New canvas** (Ctrl+N): width, height and background (white, background color or transparent).\n- **Recent files** (File ▸ Open recent): Chrome and Edge remember files you opened or saved.\n- **Install**: in Chrome or Edge use the install icon in the address bar to run Tintwork in its own window, offline.\n\n## The workspace\n\n- **Menu bar** at the top, **tool options bar** under it, **tools** on the left, **panels** on the right (Color, Histogram, Layers, History, Looks), **status bar** at the bottom.\n- **Document tabs** sit above the canvas. Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab switch, Ctrl+Alt+W closes.\n- **Navigate**: mouse wheel zooms at the cursor, hold Space or middle-drag to pan, Ctrl+0 fits, Ctrl+1 shows actual pixels.\n- Press **?** for every keyboard shortcut and **F1** for this guide.\n\n## Tools\n\n| Tool | Key | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move | V | Drag the layer, selected layers or selected pixels; arrow keys nudge (Shift = 10 px) |\n| Rectangle / Ellipse select | M / O | Drag a selection |\n| Lasso / Polygonal lasso | L / P | Freehand or point-by-point outline |\n| Magic wand | W | Select by color with a tolerance |\n| Crop | C | Drag, choose a preset ratio or size, Enter applies |\n| Straighten | | Draw along the horizon |\n| Brush / Eraser | B / E | Size, hardness, opacity; Shift+click draws a straight line |\n| Clone stamp | S | Alt+click sets the source, then paint |\n| Healing / Spot healing | R / J | Remove blemishes and dust |\n| Fill | G | Flood fill with a tolerance |\n| Text | T | Click to type; click a text layer to edit it |\n| Color picker | I | Alt+click with the brush also picks |\n| Hand / Zoom | H / Z | Pan and zoom |\n\n## Selections\n\n- Hold **Shift** to add, **Alt** to subtract, **Shift+Alt** to intersect, or use the buttons in the options bar.\n- Select ▸ All (Ctrl+A), Deselect (Ctrl+D), Invert (Ctrl+Shift+I), Feather (Shift+F6), Color range, Refine edge (Ctrl+Alt+R).\n- A selection limits painting, fills, adjustments and filters. Feathered selections blend softly.\n\n## Layers\n\n- **New layer** Ctrl+Shift+N, **duplicate** Ctrl+J, **merge down** Ctrl+E, **reorder** by dragging or Ctrl+[ and Ctrl+].\n- Each layer has visibility, opacity and a blend mode. Double-click a name to rename it.\n- **Masks**: Layer ▸ Add layer mask. Paint black to hide and white to reveal. Press `\\` to switch between mask and pixels; Shift+click the mask thumbnail to disable it.\n- **Groups** (Ctrl+G), **clipping masks** (Ctrl+Alt+G) and **layer styles** (drop shadow, inner shadow, outer and inner glow, stroke, color overlay).\n- **Multi-select**: Ctrl+click and Shift+click layers, then move, transform, align, distribute or group them together.\n- **Live layers** (Filter ▸ Convert to live layer) keep filters editable.\n\n## Adjustments and looks\n\n- **Destructive** adjustments are in the Adjust menu: Brightness/contrast (Ctrl+B), Hue/saturation (Ctrl+U), Levels (Ctrl+L), Curves (Ctrl+M), Exposure, White balance and more. They show a live preview; OK applies.\n- **Adjustment layers** (Layer ▸ New adjustment layer) never change pixels and can be edited later by double-clicking them. On big photos the preview updates at reduced resolution while you drag and sharpens a moment after you stop.\n- **Auto enhance** (Ctrl+Shift+L) and **Auto white balance** add editable layers.\n- **Looks panel**: one-click looks made of adjustment layers; Intensity sets the strength. Save your own looks and import 3D LUT (.cube) files.\n\n## Filters and transforms\n\n- Filter menu: Gaussian blur, Sharpen, Add noise, Vignette.\n- **Free transform** (Ctrl+T): scale, rotate, skew, perspective and warp grids. Enter applies, Esc cancels.\n- Image menu: rotate, flip, Image size, Canvas size, Resize to preset, Auto straighten.\n\n## Saving, exporting and recovery\n\n- **Save** (Ctrl+S) writes a `.tintwork` project that keeps layers, masks, styles, adjustment layers and 16-bit data. **Save as** is Ctrl+Shift+S.\n- **Export** (Ctrl+Shift+E): PNG, JPEG or WebP with a quality setting. **Export multiple sizes** writes several sizes at once into a folder or a ZIP.\n- **Autosave**: every open document is saved in the browser a few seconds after each change. If the browser or the tab closes unexpectedly, the next start offers to **Restore** it.\n- **Actions and batch**: record steps in the Actions menu and replay them on a folder of images with File ▸ Batch process.\n\n## Camera RAW files\n\nOpening a RAW file (File ▸ Open, drag and drop, Open recent, or double-clicking a file once Tintwork is installed) shows the **Develop** dialog with a live preview:\n\n- **Presets**: choose a saved preset, *Last used settings* or *Default settings* under **Apply**. Type a name and click **Save preset** to keep the current settings (tone curve included); **Delete** removes the chosen preset. Presets are stored in this browser. **Export presets…** saves all of them to a `.json` file, and **Import presets…** adds the presets from such a file (a preset with the same name is replaced), so you can keep a copy or move them to another computer.\n- **White balance**: *As shot* uses the camera's setting, *Auto* measures the photo, *Custom* lets you set **Temperature** (kelvin) and **Tint**. Moving either slider switches to Custom.\n- **Tone**: **Exposure** (in stops), **Highlight recovery**, **Shadows** and **Contrast**. Open **Tone curve** to drag points on the RGB curve or on the red, green or blue curve on its own; the label next to it says whether the curve is straight or edited.\n- **Color**: **Vibrance** boosts muted colours more than colours that are already strong, which is gentle on skin tones. **Saturation** changes all colours evenly; −100 gives black and white.\n- **Sharpening**: **Amount** sets the strength (0 turns it off). **Radius** is the size of the edges that are sharpened, in pixels. **Detail** at low values holds back halos on strong edges, and at high values sharpens fine texture fully. **Masking** at higher values sharpens only edges and leaves smooth areas such as skin and sky alone.\n- **Noise reduction**: **Luminance** smooths grain, **Color** removes coloured speckles. Both are a little slower to apply at full size.\n- **Lens**: the lens name and focal length from the file are shown when it records them. When the file carries correction data (most DNG files from phones and many converters), **Corrections** fixes distortion and corner darkening. Untick it to see the uncorrected image. **Distortion** straightens lines by hand: move it right when straight lines bow outward (barrel), left when they bend inward (pincushion). Tick **Grid** (or press `G`) to show a grid over the preview and line up walls, horizons or door frames against it. **Save for this lens** remembers the value for that lens at that focal length; later photos from the same lens open with it (between two saved focal lengths the value is blended), including in batch processing. **Forget** removes the saved values for the lens. Tintwork does not include a lens database, so these values are your own. **Vignette fix** brightens or darkens the corners by hand.\n- **Color fringes**: tick **Remove chromatic aberration** to measure and remove red, cyan, blue or yellow edges toward the corners automatically; the measured amount is shown next to it. **Red / cyan** and **Blue / yellow** fine-tune it by hand, or work on their own when the box is unticked.\n- **Defringe**: removes purple and green fringes along high-contrast edges, such as branches against a bright sky. **Purple amount** and **Green amount** set the strength (0 is off). The **hue from** and **hue to** sliders below each one choose the range of hues treated as fringe (in degrees). Only colours next to an edge change, so purple or green objects keep their colour. Click **Pick fringe colour** (or press `I`) and then click right on a coloured edge in the preview: Tintwork reads the fringe colour there and sets the matching hue range (and an amount of 50 if it was 0). A spot that is too neutral, or a colour that is not purple or green, is refused with a hint. Tick **Show affected areas** to see a mask instead of the photo: white is untouched, darker (tinted) pixels are the ones having their colour removed. The mask is only a view and is never applied.\n- **Local adjustments** change one part of the photo. **Add graduated** adds a graduated filter: full effect on one side of a band, fading to none on the other, like a darkened sky. **Add radial** adds an oval: the effect is inside it, or outside it with **Invert** (for a vignette around a subject). Each has **Exposure**, **Contrast** and **Temperature**; the radial filter also has **Feather** (how soft the edge is). Drag the handles in the preview with the mouse: for a graduated filter, drag the two end points or the middle; for a radial filter, drag the centre, or the right and bottom handles to change its size. Choose an adjustment under **Edit** (or click it in the preview) to change it; **Delete** (or the Delete key) removes it and `Esc` deselects. Up to eight can be added. They are part of the settings, so presets and re-developing keep them.\n- **Before / after** (or the `\\` key) shows the photo with the default settings so you can compare; click it again to return to your edit. When you re-develop a layer, *before* means the settings it was developed with.\n- **Split** (or the `Y` key) shows *before* on the left and your edit on the right. Drag the divider with the mouse to move it, or click it and use the Left and Right arrow keys. Click **Split** again to leave split view.\n- **Reset** returns to the defaults; **Use camera JPEG** opens the small, already processed JPEG stored inside the file instead.\n- **RAW file** (when opening): *Embed in the project* (the default) stores a copy of the RAW file in the project, so the project works anywhere. *Link to the file on disk* stores only a reference to the file, which keeps projects small; it needs Chrome or Edge and a file opened with File ▸ Open, Open recent or drag and drop. Your choice is remembered for the next RAW file. A linked layer shows a small ring on its RAW badge.\n- **Open** closes the dialog straight away. A quick half-resolution version appears within a moment with the label *Developing full resolution…*, then the full image replaces it as a new 16 bits/channel document. Developing is split across several background threads, so the window stays responsive; a 24 MP file is usually on screen in one to two seconds after Open.\n\nThe settings you open with become the *last used settings*.\n\n**Re-developing later**: the developed layer keeps the RAW file and its settings, and shows a small **RAW** badge in the Layers panel. Double-click its thumbnail, right-click it and choose **Re-develop RAW…**, or use Layer ▸ Re-develop RAW… to open the Develop dialog again with the settings you used. **Apply** replaces the layer's pixels with a fresh develop from the original file, in full 16-bit quality, as one step you can undo. Crops, image resizes, rotations, flips, straightening and free transforms are kept: the fresh develop is fitted into the layer exactly as it is now. If you painted on or filtered the layer after developing, you are asked first, because re-developing replaces those edits; put retouching on a separate layer to keep it. A layer whose selected pixels were transformed cannot be re-developed in place (Tintwork says so). Project files and autosave keep the RAW file (or, for linked layers, only the link), so a saved project can be re-developed after reopening; this makes the project about as much bigger as the RAW file (RAW files over 200 MB are left out of autosave). If Tintwork cannot decode a file's sensor data (an unusual camera or compression), it opens the camera's embedded JPEG instead and says so. That image is 8-bit and the develop settings do not apply.\n\n**Linked RAW files**: re-developing a linked layer reads the file from disk; the browser may ask once for permission to read it. If the file was moved, renamed or deleted, or the project was opened on another computer or in another browser, the **Linked RAW file not found** prompt names the file and its size; click **Locate file…** to choose it again (you are warned if the name or size differs). Cancel leaves the layer as it is. Layer ▸ **Embed linked RAW file** copies the file into the project so it no longer depends on the file on disk.\n\n**Batch processing** (File ▸ Batch process): **RAW files develop with** chooses the last used settings, the defaults or one of your presets for every RAW file in the batch. A batch does not change your last used settings.\n\n**Faster decoding**: when Tintwork is hosted with cross-origin isolation headers (as the included hosting settings do), the RAW decoder uses several threads and decoding takes about half as long. Otherwise it uses one thread; nothing else changes.\n\n**Offline**: the RAW decoder is downloaded and stored when Tintwork is first loaded, so RAW files open without a connection even if you have never opened one before.\n\n## Layered PSD files\n\n- **Open** a `.psd` or `.psb` file to get its layers, names, opacity, visibility, blend modes, groups, clipping and layer masks, and adjustment layers that Tintwork also has (brightness/contrast, levels, curves, exposure, hue/saturation, invert). 16-bit files stay 16-bit.\n- Some parts are converted: text, smart objects and fill or shape layers become pixel layers using the pixels stored in the file, and layer effects are left off. Blend modes Tintwork does not have use the closest match, and adjustment types it does not have are skipped. A notice lists everything that was converted. If the layers cannot be read, the flattened image stored in the file opens instead.\n- **Export** (File ▸ Export layered PSD…, or PSD in the Export dialog) writes layers, groups, masks, clipping, blend modes, opacity, matching adjustment layers and a flattened copy for other apps. Layer styles are baked into their layer's pixels, text and live layers are written as pixels, and 3D LUT and white balance layers are left out (a notice says which). Batch processing can write PSD too.\n\n## 16-bit editing\n\nOpen a 16-bit PNG, a 16-bit PSD or a camera RAW file, or choose Image ▸ Mode: 16 bits/channel. Painting, adjustments, filters, transforms, groups, masks and every layer style keep 16-bit precision. Export to PNG or PSD keeps 16 bits; JPEG and WebP are 8-bit.\n\n## Large images and memory\n\nTintwork estimates how much memory a document needs (width × height × layers). Very large canvases show a warning; ones too big for the browser are refused, and an image that is too large to open is offered at a reduced size. Close unused tabs and flatten finished groups to save memory. Up to 16384 × 16384 pixels is supported.\n\n## Performance and the rendering engine\n\nView ▸ Rendering engine chooses WebGPU, WebGL2 or the CPU fallback. Help ▸ Run performance benchmark measures your hardware and reports the times. If something renders wrongly, switching to the CPU engine is the safest fallback.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\n- Errors show a short message at the bottom of the window and your work is autosaved immediately. Reload the page and choose **Restore** if anything looks wrong.\n- Files that cannot be read (damaged or unsupported) are reported by name; nothing else is changed.\n- Recent files that were moved or deleted are removed from the list with a notice.\n\n## Accessibility\n\nEvery control can be reached with the keyboard (Tab and Shift+Tab); the focused control has a visible outline. Buttons with only an icon have a spoken name. Dialogs open with the first field focused; Enter confirms and Esc cancels.\n";
